# Port the API server listens on (defaults to 3000)
PORT=3000
# Port the admin Vite dev server listens on (defaults to 4200). Also the origin
# the server's ALLOWED_ORIGINS defaults to, and the one admin-e2e drives.
ADMIN_PORT=4200
# Where the admin dev server proxies /api. Defaults to PORT, which is what you
# want unless the admin is pointed at an API from another checkout.
API_PORT=
# PostgreSQL connection string
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://apograph:apograph@localhost:5432/apograph_cms
# PORT, ADMIN_PORT and the database name are what a parallel worktree stack
# varies — one running stack per ticket, so several can be live at once.
# Provision one with `npm run worktree -- provision <slot>`; the slot table and
# the memory ceiling are in docs/parallel-stacks.md.
# Serve the Scalar API reference on /reference (OpenAPI JSON on /reference/json).
# Unset, it is on outside production. Set true to publish it from a deployment.
API_DOCS=
# How many reverse proxies sit in front of the server (Express `trust proxy`).
# REQUIRED behind a load balancer, ingress or CDN: unset, Express ignores
# X-Forwarded-For and every request reports the proxy's address, so the login
# rate limit collapses into ONE bucket for the whole deployment — one attacker's
# 10 requests a minute then deny login to every user, and every session row
# records the proxy's IP instead of the client's.
# Prefer the hop count (1 for a single proxy); a client cannot forge past it.
# Also accepts true (trust the whole X-Forwarded-For chain — only on a network
# where nothing untrusted reaches the app directly) or a subnet/preset list
# passed to Express verbatim, e.g. loopback or 10.0.0.0/8.
# Leave unset when the server is exposed directly.
TRUST_PROXY=
# Largest JSON/urlencoded request body the API accepts, in express's `bytes`
# syntax (1mb, 512kb) or a raw byte count. Defaults to 1mb. Raise it only if
# your entries are genuinely larger — a body over the cap is refused with a
# bare 413 by the body parser, before any controller or guard runs. Media
# uploads are multipart and unaffected; see MEDIA_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES.
MAX_REQUEST_BODY=

# --- identity plugin ---
# No signing secret: sessions and one-time invite/reset tokens are opaque
# 256-bit random values checked against a row, not signed blobs. There is
# nothing to configure and nothing to rotate — revoking a session is deleting
# its row. (SESSION_SECRET / TOKEN_SECRET used to be listed here as REQUIRED and
# were read by nothing.)
# Optional lifetime overrides, in seconds
SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=604800
INVITE_TTL_SECONDS=604800
RESET_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# --- single sign-on ---
# The identity providers themselves are registered in apps/server/src/plugins.ts
# (they are constructed adapters, not environment values). These two settings
# shape the handshake.
#
# The origin browsers reach this API on, e.g. https://cms.example.com. It builds
# the redirect_uri you register with each provider, and it is configured rather
# than read from the request's Host header, which a client controls. Leave it
# empty when the admin and the API share an origin — the usual case, and the dev
# setup where Vite proxies /api — and the first ALLOWED_ORIGINS entry is used.
SSO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# How long one sign-in attempt stays live, in seconds. Default 600: a consent
# screen plus a second factor, and no longer.
SSO_REQUEST_TTL_SECONDS=600

# The identity provider itself is registered in apps/server/src/plugins.ts.
# These two are what make it configured at all — an issuer with no client id is
# a sign-in button that can only fail. Generic OpenID Connect: Okta, Auth0,
# Keycloak, Google, Entra ID, Authentik, Zitadel and the rest all speak it.
#
# The issuer must match the `iss` claim byte for byte, trailing slash included
# (Auth0 issues with one). Register
# <SSO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL>/api/auth/sso/<SSO_OIDC_NAME>/callback with the provider.
SSO_OIDC_ISSUER=
SSO_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
SSO_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# What the route and every sso_identities row call this provider. Renaming it
# orphans the links that name it. Default: oidc
SSO_OIDC_NAME=
# The sign-in button's text. Defaults to the issuer's host.
SSO_OIDC_LABEL=
# Comma-separated. Default: openid,profile,email
SSO_OIDC_SCOPES=
# Set to true ONLY if your provider never sends `email_verified` and you are
# asserting that this directory owns the addresses it reports. Microsoft Entra
# ID is the common case. The claim is the only gate on a first sign-in claiming
# an existing account, so this is an operator's decision, never a default.
SSO_OIDC_EMAIL_VERIFIED_WHEN_ABSENT=

# --- single sign-on: authority ---
# Just-in-time provisioning. Comma-separated email domains an account may be
# CREATED for on first sign-in. Empty (the default) means SSO creates nobody and
# only signs in accounts that already exist — Apograph is invite-only, and SSO
# replaces the credential check rather than the way in.
#
# Setting this without a domain list is not possible on purpose: an identity
# provider answers for everyone it knows, and a public one knows everyone.
SSO_PROVISION_DOMAINS=
# The role a provisioned account lands on. Default: viewer
SSO_PROVISION_ROLE=
# Set to false to make the identity provider the only way in. The root admin
# (APOGRAPH_ROOT_ADMIN_EMAIL) keeps a password regardless — without that exemption,
# a mis-scoped provider locks you out of your own CMS.
SSO_ALLOW_PASSWORD_LOGIN=
# Lifetime for sessions opened through an identity provider, in seconds.
# Defaults to SESSION_TTL_SECONDS. Worth shortening for a provider with no
# back-channel logout: without it, the session's own expiry is the only thing
# that eventually ends access after someone is offboarded.
SSO_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=

# --- single sign-on: GitHub ---
# GitHub is OAuth2, not OIDC, so it is its own adapter rather than a preset.
# Both credentials are required — there is no PKCE on this flow, so the secret
# is the only thing proving the code is being redeemed by this application.
SSO_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
SSO_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
SSO_GITHUB_NAME=
SSO_GITHUB_LABEL=
# A GitHub Enterprise Server root, e.g. https://github.acme.com. Empty for
# github.com.
SSO_GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_URL=
# Shapes GitHub's account chooser only. The CMS's own rules decide who gets in.
SSO_GITHUB_ORG=

# --- single sign-on: SAML 2.0 ---
# The IdP's single-sign-on URL and its signing certificate. SAML has no
# discovery document and no key endpoint: the certificate IS the trust
# relationship, so both are required and a rollover is a config change.
SSO_SAML_ENTRY_POINT=
SSO_SAML_IDP_CERT=
SSO_SAML_NAME=
SSO_SAML_LABEL=
# The entity id your IdP has registered for this application. Defaults to
# SSO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
SSO_SAML_ISSUER=
# Set when the IdP's NameID is an email address: point this at an immutable
# directory id instead. An address follows a mailbox, not a person.
SSO_SAML_SUBJECT_ATTRIBUTE=
SSO_SAML_EMAIL_ATTRIBUTE=
SSO_SAML_GROUPS_ATTRIBUTE=
# SAML carries NO verification claim at all. Setting this to true is you
# asserting that your directory owns the addresses it reports — which is the
# only gate on a first sign-in claiming an existing account.
SSO_SAML_EMAIL_VERIFIED=

# --- media plugin ---
# Which storage backend runs is NOT an environment variable: it is the provider
# `apps/server/src/plugins.ts` constructs. These are that provider's settings.
# Directory local blobs are written under. Git-ignored by default; point this at
# a persistent volume for a real deployment (a fresh container's disk is wiped).
MEDIA_LOCAL_ROOT=./.storage/media
# Max upload size in bytes (default 50 MB).
MEDIA_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=52428800
# How a download reaches the browser. `off` (default) streams every byte
# through the app. `signed-url` redirects an already-authorized download
# straight to the storage backend — only possible on a backend that can sign
# one, so with the default filesystem provider this is a boot error, on
# purpose.
MEDIA_DIRECT_SERVE=off
MEDIA_DIRECT_SERVE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# --- copilot plugin ---
# Global kill switch. OFF by default (ADR-0005 §10): the copilot is a large
# feature, and whether a deployment offers it at all is the operator's call.
# `false` unregisters every /api/copilot route, so the endpoints 404 and the
# admin's chat panel, Agents view and Skills page stand down — off means off,
# not a chat that refuses when you press send.
#
# It is NOT what keeps content in-house: a backend is registered only when it
# is configured below, so a deployment with no key reaches no third party
# whatever this says.
COPILOT_ENABLED=false
# There is no "which provider" setting. A backend is registered only when it is
# configured below, in the order plugins.ts lists them (claude, then the
# OpenAI-wire one), and the FIRST registered one serves a run that names none —
# which is also what the admin's model picker opens on. Configure nothing and
# there is no catalogue and no copilot: setting COPILOT_ENABLED=true with no
# backend configured refuses to boot rather than shipping a chat that cannot
# answer.
# Ceiling on a single model response, in tokens.
COPILOT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192
# The three run ceilings. A run that exceeds any one of them stops with a reason
# the chat shows, so an answer that ends in "This answer is incomplete" is
# asking for a bigger number here. Raising them costs tokens, not safety: every
# step is still authorized and audited, and a proposed write is still recorded
# before it is applied.
#
# Move them TOGETHER. They are checked in the same loop, so lifting one alone
# relocates the wall rather than removing it — a run given more steps and the
# same wall clock just stops on "ran longer than allowed" instead.
#
# Model calls one run may make. A "step" is a model call plus any tools it asks
# for, so this bounds the tool loop's depth, not its width. Defaults to 30. A
# smaller local model needs more round trips than a frontier one to answer the
# same question.
COPILOT_MAX_STEPS=
# Wall clock for the whole run, in milliseconds. Defaults to 300000 (5 min).
# This is what a user waits, so it is the ceiling to be conservative with.
COPILOT_WALL_CLOCK_MS=
# Input + output tokens across every model call in one run. Defaults to 400000.
# The ceiling that actually bounds spend — raise it deliberately.
COPILOT_MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS=
# Native Claude. Setting the key is what REGISTERS this backend — leave it
# empty and there is no `claude` in the picker at all, rather than one that
# fails on the first message. The key never leaves the server.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Comma-separated. The FIRST is the default; the rest are what a user can
# switch to mid-conversation without a redeploy.
COPILOT_ANTHROPIC_MODELS=claude-opus-5,claude-sonnet-5,claude-haiku-4-5
# Optional: a gateway, proxy or regional endpoint instead of api.anthropic.com.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# OpenAI-wire-format endpoint. Setting this REGISTERS the `ollama` backend, and
# there is no default: an endpoint nobody named is a backend that can only time
# out. A local Ollama is http://localhost:11434/v1 — or point it at vLLM,
# llama.cpp, LM Studio, LiteLLM, Azure or OpenAI.
COPILOT_OPENAI_BASE_URL=
# Comma-separated, first is the default.
COPILOT_OPENAI_MODELS=llama3.1
# Leave empty for a local runtime that wants no auth.
COPILOT_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- mcp plugin (the CMS as a Model Context Protocol server) ---
# Global kill switch. OFF by default (ADR-0006): enabling it lets any holder of
# a `full`-scope API token drive content CRUD from an external agent, so an
# operator opts in explicitly. Tokens, scopes and workspace buckets are
# unchanged — this only controls whether the MCP front door is mounted.
# Once on, agents connect to POST /api/v1/mcp with `Authorization: Bearer
# <token>` (mint one in the admin's API Tokens page — `read` scope for a
# retrieval-only agent, `full` for one that authors).
MCP_ENABLED=false

# --- webhooks plugin (outgoing notifications on content changes) ---
# Endpoints themselves live in the database — they are operational data, added
# in the admin's Webhooks page (directory group, administrator-only). What is
# here is how hard the sender pushes and where it is allowed to reach.
#
# How often the sender looks for queued deliveries, in milliseconds. Set it to
# 0 to queue but never send from this process — which is how a deployment
# dedicates one node to outgoing traffic while the web nodes only enqueue.
WEBHOOKS_DELIVERY_INTERVAL=2000
# Per-request timeout, in milliseconds.
WEBHOOKS_TIMEOUT=10000
# How long a finished delivery stays in the log. Nothing else prunes this
# table, so a 0 here is a deliberate choice to let it grow forever.
WEBHOOKS_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Permit http:// destinations. A signature over plaintext still leaves the
# payload readable to anyone on the network path, so this is for local
# development, not for a deployment.
WEBHOOKS_ALLOW_INSECURE_URLS=false
# Permit loopback, link-local and RFC 1918 destinations. OFF by default because
# a webhook is, precisely, "the server makes a request to an address a user
# typed" — the shape of every SSRF, and the reason the cloud metadata endpoint
# at 169.254.169.254 is refused. Turn it on only for a self-hosted install whose
# receiver genuinely sits inside the same network.
WEBHOOKS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORKS=false

# --- mail (off by default) ---
# The CMS sends nothing until MAIL_PROVIDER names a backend. That is a complete
# configuration, and the one a fresh clone runs: no queue, no worker, and the
# invite and password-reset routes hand the raw link back for an administrator
# to relay by hand, exactly as they always have.
#
# Setting it changes the API on purpose: with a mailer configured those routes
# stop returning the token, because two copies of a link that can take over an
# account are worse than one (ADR-0018). `POST /api/users/:id/reveal-link`
# (users:manage, audited) is how an administrator sees one when a message did
# not arrive.
#
# `smtp` is the real answer — Resend, SES, Postmark, SendGrid, Mailgun, Google
# Workspace, Microsoft 365 and any relay inside a perimeter all speak it.
# `console` writes messages to the server log instead of sending them: it is for
# reading an invite link during development, and never for a deployment, where
# it would make invitations look sent and reach nobody.
MAIL_PROVIDER=
# REQUIRED whenever MAIL_PROVIDER is set: the absolute URL the admin is served
# from. Every link in every message is built from it, and it is deliberately not
# taken from the request's Host header — the caller controls that, and an
# invitation is the one message a recipient is certain to click.
APP_URL=
# REQUIRED whenever MAIL_PROVIDER is set. A bare address or a "Name <address>"
# pair, whichever your relay accepts on behalf of the sending domain.
MAIL_FROM=
# Where replies go, when that is not MAIL_FROM.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=
# What the copy calls the product. Only worth setting if you renamed the CMS for
# your editors.
MAIL_PRODUCT_NAME=
# Return the raw link in the API response *as well as* sending the message. A
# delivery-debugging mode: it puts a second copy of an account-takeover secret
# into an HTTP response, so turn it off again once you have found the problem.
MAIL_REVEAL_LINKS=false
# How often the sender looks for queued messages, in milliseconds. Unset uses
# the default (2000).
MAIL_DELIVERY_INTERVAL_MS=
# How many hand-off attempts a message gets before it becomes a dead letter.
MAIL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=
# --- SMTP relay (only read when MAIL_PROVIDER=smtp) ---
SMTP_HOST=
# Defaults to 587 (submission, STARTTLS). Use 465 for implicit TLS and set
# SMTP_SECURE=true with it.
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_SECURE=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Refuse a relay that cannot upgrade to TLS. Leave unset inside a perimeter
# where the relay has no certificate; set it to true for anything reachable from
# the internet.
SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=
SMTP_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=

# --- root admin bootstrap (self-hosted) ---
# When APOGRAPH_ROOT_ADMIN_EMAIL is set, an active admin is provisioned on boot —
# idempotent and non-destructive (an existing account is left untouched).
# Leave the email unset to skip. The password is bcrypt-hashed before storage.
APOGRAPH_ROOT_ADMIN_EMAIL=
APOGRAPH_ROOT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- releasing to npm (only needed to run `npm run release`) ---
# Read by tools/release/release.mjs; nothing else in the app looks at these.
# An npm automation token with publish rights on the @apograph scope. Leave it
# empty to publish as whoever `npm login` logged in as instead.
NPM_TOKEN=
# A GitHub token with `repo` access on this repository — `nx release` creates
# the GitHub Release with it. The release refuses to start without one, because
# the version is committed, tagged and pushed before that step is reached.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
